    "narrative": "Today\u2019s optimal lineup for Ghost Ride the WHIP is anchored by **James Wood**, whose 78.42 efficiency score makes him our top play. Facing Chandler, **James Wood** benefits from a high-upside matchup against a sub-5.00 xERA starter and his elite 0.420 xwOBA, which, combined with the leadoff spot and a favorable stolen base environment against a slow-delivering pitcher, creates an elite ceiling. He is joined in the top tier by **Mickey Moniak** (75.75), who thrives on a massive +14% platoon edge against Mahle, and **Brice Turang** (73.40), who leverages an aggressive stolen base opportunity and a strong leadoff position to maximize his output against Rodriguez.\n\nThe situational mapping heavily favors hitters in favorable parks today, most notably **Kyle Karros**, whose score is inflated by a significant +14% park factor that helps offset the inherent risk of a right-on-right matchup. We also find meaningful value in **Seiya Suzuki** (67.40) and **Isaac Paredes** (66.30), both of whom capitalize on soft SP matchups to secure starting roles. **Corbin Carroll** (73.03) remains a foundational start, though his potential for stolen bases is slightly tempered today by the scouting report on the opposing catcher.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Cole Young**, who landed just outside the cut with a 49.98 score. While his situational profile against Yesavage is serviceable, the optimizer favored the higher volatility and upside of **Alex Freeland**, whose score of 55.54 is driven almost entirely by the favorable SP matchup. We are closely monitoring weather reports for any late-breaking developments, but with no significant threats, the main risk to this lineup is the marginal output from **Max Muncy** (53.64); he holds the lowest score in our active lineup, but his park factor and the potential for a rare stolen base against P\u00e9rez kept him ahead of the likes of **Austin Riley** and **Travis Bazzana** on our depth chart.",
